<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Feng Ba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 430</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Emperor Wencheng of Northern Yan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Feng Ba, known as Emperor Wencheng of Northern Yan, was a general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> family in the service of Later Yan. In 407 he and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-yun">Gao Yun</a> killed the tyrannical emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xi">Murong Xi</a> at Longcheng and made Gao Yun ruler. After Gao Yun was murdered in 409, Feng Ba took the throne himself, and his family ruled Northern Yan until the Northern Wei conquest of 436.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Feng Ba's grandfather Feng He, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>, settled in Shangdang Commandery, and his father Feng An served the Western Yan emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-yong">Murong Yong</a> as a general. When Later Yan destroyed Western Yan in 394 the household was moved to Helong, also called Longcheng, where Feng Ba grew up under strong <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianbei">Xianbei</a> influence, as his Xianbei nickname Qizhifa suggests. Unlike his three younger brothers, who ignored social restraints, he was thought careful and diligent. He became a general under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-bao">Murong Bao</a> and a close friend of Murong Bao's adoptive son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-yun">Murong Yun</a>.</p><p>In 407 he and his brother Feng Sufu offended the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-xi">Murong Xi</a> and hid in the countryside, then rose inside Longcheng while the emperor was away burying his wife, and persuaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-yun">Murong Yun</a>, who soon took back his original name Gao Yun, to lead them. Gao Yun made Feng Ba prime minister, and the government was effectively in his hands.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Feng Ba made Feng Sufu prime minister and gave high posts to his kinsmen and allies. He and Feng Sufu were regarded as diligent, frugal and intelligent, and Northern Yan was thought well governed. In 410 his cousin Feng Wani and Feng Ruchen rebelled; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-hong">Feng Hong</a> defeated them and executed them after their surrender. In 411 Feng Ba married his daughter Princess Lelang to the Rouran khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yujiulu-hulu">Yujiulü Hulü</a>, and in 414 he brought back 5,000 households of his clan from Changle.</p><p>He refused the submission demanded by the Northern Wei envoy Huniuyu Shimen, imprisoning but sparing him, and allied instead with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helian-bobo">Helian Bobo</a> of Xia. In 415 he executed three brothers of the general Sun Hu for complaining and then poisoned Sun Hu himself. In 418 the Northern Wei emperor Mingyuan besieged Helong; Feng Ba held the city, but the Wei carried off some 10,000 households.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Seriously ill in 430, Feng Ba transferred his authority to his crown prince Feng Yi, but his favourite, Consort Song, wanted the throne for her own son Feng Shouju and cut him off from the court with orders issued in his name. Her official Hu Fu warned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feng-hong">Feng Hong</a>, who seized the palace; Feng Ba died of the shock, and Feng Hong took the throne and killed all his sons. Historians disagree whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gao-yun">Gao Yun</a> or Feng Ba should count as the first ruler of Northern Yan.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Feng Ba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The last emperor, the extravagant Murong Xi, was killed in 407 by <b>Feng Ba</b> and Gao Yun, who founded Northern Yan.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-yan">Later Yan</a></li><li><blockquote>In 407 the general <b>Feng Ba</b> and Gao Yun, a man of Goguryeo descent adopted into the Later Yan royal family, killed the tyrannical emperor Murong Xi at Longcheng (Chaoyang, Liaoning).</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-yan">Northern Yan</a></li></ul></section>
